Thought: The error indicates some of the columns I guessed (like Title/Description) don’t exist, so I need to (1) inspect the structure of relevant tables, (2) find where the filename `winatp-intro-backdoor.exe` appears (likely in AlertEvidence or DeviceFileEvents), (3) identify the alert/incident and device/time where PowerShell dropped that file, (4) find the subsequent alert about a suspicious PowerShell download or encoded command for the same device/incident, and (5) pivot to DeviceProcessEvents to get the PowerShell process ID and command line.

First I’ll describe key tables (SecurityAlert, AlertInfo, AlertEvidence, DeviceFileEvents, DeviceProcessEvents) to see available columns. Then I’ll search for the filename in AlertEvidence/DeviceFileEvents, use the associated AlertId/IncidentId and DeviceId, find the related PowerShell alert, and finally pull the corresponding DeviceProcessEvents entry (ProcessCommandLine containing powershell with download/encoded command) to get the PID and command line.

